        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>OBJECTIVES:</B><BR>
Study 1: The purpose of the first study is to investigate the effect of paralysis on bone density and structural parameters of the tibia.  Study 2: The purpose of the second study was to investigate bone mass and structure in the tibia after resection of the fibula.
<P>
<B>APPROACH:</B><BR>
Study 1:  were two male and  two female adults aged 17-32 years who had sustained a spinal cord injury at least 4 years prior to the study.  Cross-sectional structural parameters including area (A), principal area moments of inertia (Imax, Imin), polar area moments of inertia (J), and major principal axis orientation of the tibia and fibula were computed every millimeter along the scan length for the three scans of the leg using the lower energy for its larger dynamic range.  Study 2: Subjects who had undergone a fibular resection at least 2 years earlier were recruited (n=3).  Fibular resection was carried out in all 3 men to remedy avascular necrosis in the hip.  In addition to anthropomorphic data (BW, Ht, tibial length) a set of 3 scans of the tibia on right and left sides as above was done as well as a DXA scan of the right and left calcaneus to measure areal bone density, an indication of loading symmetry. Structural parameters of the tibia and fibula as enumerated above were performed on one leg and on the tibia of the other leg. 
<P>
<B>RESULTS:</B><BR>
Study 1: Body weights in paraplegics were greater than in controls.  Two male subjects had lower values for cross-sectional areas and density as well as lower structural parameters in the midshaft, distal tibia and femoral midshaft.  Data supporting bone loss in the two female paraplegics was not convincing except at the tibial midshaft where the data was not scaled to body size.  In one male subject, cross sectional areas and principal moments of inertia of the tibia were not symmetrical.  Conclusions of study 1: The large variation in results of just a few subjects point to the need for longitudinal studies and more subjects to definitively measure changes in structural parameters due to paralysis and unloading.  Study 2: Subject 1. The cross-sectional area of the tibia on the side of the fibular resection (rt) was increased 2% at the midshaft and 33% at the distal third compared to the unaffected tibia.  The principal and polar moments of inertia on the right tibia were increased 51% and 89% at the midshaft and distal third, respectively.  The midshaft was also more symmetric, but not the distal section.  Subject 2: The tibia on the resected side (left) showed an increase in cross-sectional area (8% at midshaft and 10% at distal third) and an increased minimum moment of inertia of 22 % in the mid and 47% in the distal third.  Imax increased by 9% in both sites.  Subject 3:  The increases in the cross-sectional area were 32% at the mid and 27% at the distal third, compared to the unaffected left side.  Imin increased by 74% and 95% at the distal third and Imax increased 15 and 24 % in the same respective regions, an indication the tibia is more symmetric. Calcaneal density was not consistent in the 3 subjects and showed R and L differences outside the 0.6% observed in normal men. Calcaneal density was less (by 5.7 and 8 %) in the affected than the unaffected limb in two subjects who also had functional problems in gait.  The lower heel density may be an indication of less load on the affected limb and was not present in the subject who reported normal gait and skiing activity post surgery.  Conclusions: All three subjects with fibular resection showed an increase in cross-sectional area as well as structural parameters related to stiffness and strength, an indication of increased loading of the tibia from the ipsilateral side of the fibular resection resulting in increased bone deposition.  This points to a role of the fibula in driving the shape of the tibia cross-section and in load bearing.<END>
